[{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q1","number":1,"question":"<p><strong>The following questions only require AS Maths Integration methods.</strong></p><p>Explain why each of the following are improper integrals.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\sqrt{x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\frac{1}{3x^3}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>The upper limit is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the integral has an infinite limit and is improper.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>The integrand <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/(3x^3)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, which is one of the limits, so the integral is improper.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q2","number":2,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^3}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^4}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1{x^2}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\int_2^a x^{-2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac1x\\right]_2^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac1a+\\frac12\\right)=\\frac12.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1{x^3}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac1{2x^2}\\right]_2^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac1{2a^2}+\\frac18\\right)=\\frac18.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1{x^4}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac1{3x^3}\\right]_2^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac1{3a^3}+\\frac1{24}\\right)=\\frac1{24}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/a^2\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q3","number":3,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\frac{1}{x^3}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\frac{1}{x^4}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^2\\frac1{x^2}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[-\\frac1x\\right]_a^2\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left(-\\frac12+\\frac1a\\right)=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so the integral diverges.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^2\\frac1{x^3}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[-\\frac1{2x^2}\\right]_a^2\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left(-\\frac18+\\frac1{2a^2}\\right)=+\\infty.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Therefore the integral diverges.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^2\\frac1{x^4}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[-\\frac1{3x^3}\\right]_a^2\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left(-\\frac1{24}+\\frac1{3a^3}\\right)=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so the integral diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q4","number":4,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^9\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_4^\\infty\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^9\\frac1{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[2\\sqrt{x}\\right]_a^9\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}(6-2\\sqrt a)=6.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_4^\\infty\\frac1{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[2\\sqrt{x}\\right]_4^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}(2\\sqrt a-4)=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so the integral diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q5","number":5,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^8\\frac{1}{x^{3/4}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_8^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^{3/4}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^8x^{-3/4}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[4x^{1/4}\\right]_a^8\n=4\\sqrt[4]{8}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_8^\\infty x^{-3/4}\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[4x^{1/4}\\right]_8^a\n=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so the integral diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q6","number":6,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_4^\\infty192x^{-4}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_4^\\infty(192x^{-4}+1)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_4^\\infty192x^{-4}(x+1)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_4^\\infty192x^{-4}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-64x^{-3}\\right]_4^a\n=0-(-1)=1.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_4^\\infty(192x^{-4}+1)\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-64x^{-3}+x\\right]_4^a=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so it diverges.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_4^\\infty192x^{-4}(x+1)\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\int_4^a(192x^{-3}+192x^{-4})\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-96x^{-2}-64x^{-3}\\right]_4^a=6+1=7.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q7","number":7,"question":"<p>Show clearly that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^4\\frac{1}{x^3\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span> diverges.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^4\\frac1{x^3\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\int_a^4x^{-7/2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[-\\frac25x^{-5/2}\\right]_a^4\n=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so it diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q8","number":8,"question":"<p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1/64}\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac1{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\right)\\,dx=\\frac5{32}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^{1/64}\\left(x^{-1/2}-x^{-1/3}\\right)\\,dx\n=\\left[2\\sqrt{x}-\\frac32x^{2/3}\\right]_0^{1/64}\n=\\frac14-\\frac3{32}=\\frac5{32}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q9","number":9,"question":"<p>Amelia is attempting to evaluate the integral <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^2\\frac{35}{x^6}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Amelia's working is shown below:</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-2}^2\\frac{35}{x^6}\\,dx\n=\\int_{-2}^2 35x^{-6}\\,dx\n=[-7x^{-5}]_{-2}^2\n=((-7\\times2^{-5})-(-7\\times(-2)^{-5}))\n=-\\frac7{32}-\\frac7{32}\n=-\\frac7{16}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Explain the error in Amelia's method and what she should do instead.</p></li><li><p>Show clearly that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^2\\frac{35}{x^6}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span> diverges.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>Amelia's method is incorrect because <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"35/x^6\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so this is an improper integral. She should split it at a value <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\" data-display=\"false\"></span> tending to zero, evaluate the two resulting integrals as limits, and then test whether both limits converge.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-2}^{2}\\frac{35}{x^6}\\,dx\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}\\int_{-2}^{a}35x^{-6}\\,dx\n+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\int_a^{2}35x^{-6}\\,dx\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}\\left[-7x^{-5}\\right]_{-2}^{a}\n+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[-7x^{-5}\\right]_{a}^{2}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}\\left(-7a^{-5}-\\frac7{32}\\right)\n+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left(-\\frac7{32}+7a^{-5}\\right).\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\\to0^-\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-7a^{-5}\\to+\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\\to0^+\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"7a^{-5}\\to+\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The integral diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q10","number":10,"question":"<p>Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^8\\frac4{x^{2/3}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>The integrand is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so split the integral there:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-1}^8\\frac4{x^{2/3}}\\,dx\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}\\left[12x^{1/3}\\right]_{-1}^{a}\n+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[12x^{1/3}\\right]_{a}^{8}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}(12a^{1/3}+12)+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}(24-12a^{1/3})\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =12+24=36.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q11","number":11,"question":"<p>Find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-32}^1\\frac{100}{x^{4/5}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. <strong>The following questions require A-Level Maths Integration methods, including the Integrals of Standard Functions, Reversing the Chain Rule, Integration by Substitution, Integration by Parts, and Partial Fractions.</strong></p>","steps":["<p>The integrand is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so split the integral there:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-32}^1\\frac{100}{x^{4/5}}\\,dx\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}\\left[500x^{1/5}\\right]_{-32}^{a}\n+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\left[500x^{1/5}\\right]_{a}^{1}\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =\\lim_{a\\to0^-}(500a^{1/5}+1000)\n+\\lim_{a\\to0^+}(500-500a^{1/5})\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n =1000+500=1500.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q12","number":12,"question":"<p>Explain why each of the following are improper integrals.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_4^6\\frac1{x-5}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\frac{e^x}{e^x-2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2\\pi/3}\\frac{\\sqrt3\\sin x+\\cos x}{\\sin x-\\sqrt3\\cos x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1x^4\\ln x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/(x-5)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=5\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, which lies between the limits <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"6\" data-display=\"false\"></span>; hence this is improper.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^x/(e^x-2)\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is undefined when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^x=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\ln2\\approx0.693\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, which is between <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>; hence this is improper.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">c)</h3><p>The denominator vanishes when <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sin x-\\sqrt3\\cos x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tan x=\\sqrt3\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\pi/3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. This lies between <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\pi/3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the integral is improper.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">d)</h3><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\ln x\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, which is one of the limits; hence the integral is improper.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q13","number":13,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^2\\frac1x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1x\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}[\\ln x]_2^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}(\\ln a-\\ln2)=+\\infty.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Therefore it diverges.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^2\\frac1x\\,dx=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}[\\ln x]_a^2\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}(\\ln2-\\ln a)=+\\infty,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so it diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q14","number":14,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty\\frac1{e^x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty\\frac1{e^{2x}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac1{e^x}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}(1-e^{-a})=1.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac1{e^{2x}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac12e^{-2x}\\right]_0^a\n=\\frac12.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q15","number":15,"question":"<p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_7^\\infty(x-3)^{-5/2}\\,dx=\\frac1{12}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_7^\\infty(x-3)^{-5/2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac23(x-3)^{-3/2}\\right]_7^a\n=\\frac{2}{3\\cdot8}=\\frac1{12}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q16","number":16,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{62.5}\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^6}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>The integrand is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=-3/2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so split the interval there. The two limits contain the term</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int\\frac2{(2x+3)^6}\\,dx=-\\frac1{5(2x+3)^5}+c.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>As <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to-3/2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> from either side, the corresponding improper integral tends to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"+\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence it diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q17","number":17,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_3^4\\frac{x}{x^2-9}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_3^4\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2-9}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_3^4\\frac{x}{x^2-9}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to3^+}\\left[\\frac12\\ln|x^2-9|\\right]_a^4\n=\\lim_{a\\to3^+}\\frac12\\left(\\ln7-\\ln(a^2-9)\\right)=+\\infty.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus it diverges.</p>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_3^4\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2-9}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to3^+}\\left[\\sqrt{x^2-9}\\right]_a^4\n=\\sqrt7.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q18","number":18,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty4xe^{-x^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty4xe^{-x^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty4xe^{-x^2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-2e^{-x^2}\\right]_0^a=2.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>Split at zero:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}4xe^{-x^2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to-\\infty}\\left[-2e^{-x^2}\\right]_a^0\n+\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\left[-2e^{-x^2}\\right]_0^b=-2+2=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q19","number":19,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^0\\frac{e^x}{e^x+1}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-\\infty}^0\\frac{e^x}{e^x+1}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to-\\infty}\\left[\\ln(e^x+1)\\right]_a^0\n=\\ln2-\\lim_{a\\to-\\infty}\\ln(1+e^a)=\\ln2.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q20","number":20,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to(\\pi/2)^-}\\left[-\\ln|\\cos x|\\right]_0^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to(\\pi/2)^-}-\\ln(\\cos a)=+\\infty.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>It diverges.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q21","number":21,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{8-2x-x^2}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>Since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"8-2x-x^2=9-(x+1)^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, the denominator is zero at the upper limit <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=8-2x-x^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-1}^2\\frac{x+1}{\\sqrt{8-2x-x^2}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to2^-}\\left[-\\sqrt{8-2x-x^2}\\right]_{-1}^{a}\n=3.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q22","number":22,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{6a-5}{3a+7}\\right)=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_4^\\infty\\left(\\frac6{6x-5}-\\frac3{3x+7}\\right)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\frac{6a-5}{3a+7}\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\frac{6-5/a}{3+7/a}=2.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_4^\\infty\\left(\\frac6{6x-5}-\\frac3{3x+7}\\right)dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[\\ln|6x-5|-\\ln|3x+7|\\right]_4^a.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, both logarithm arguments equal <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"19\" data-display=\"false\"></span>; as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(6a-5)/(3a+7)\\to2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> by part a. Thus the limit is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\ln2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q23","number":23,"question":"<p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}\\,dx=\\ln k\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is a rational number to be found.</p>","steps":["<p>Using partial fractions,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac2{x(3x+1)}=\\frac2x-\\frac6{3x+1}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{3x-2}{x(3x+1)}\\,dx\n=\\int_1^\\infty\\left(\\frac2x-\\frac6{3x+1}\\right)dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[2\\ln x-2\\ln(3x+1)\\right]_1^a\n=\\ln\\frac{16}{9},\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=16/9\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q24","number":24,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2\\frac{2-x}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>Put <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=\\sqrt{x+1}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=u^2-1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"dx=2u\\,du\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, and the limits become <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt3\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-1}^2\\frac{2-x}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,dx\n=\\int_0^{\\sqrt3}2(3-u^2)\\,du\n=\\left[6u-\\frac23u^3\\right]_0^{\\sqrt3}=4\\sqrt3.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q25","number":25,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{1/\\pi}^\\infty\\frac{\\sin(1/x)}{x^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=1/x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"dx=-du/u^2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the limits become <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{1/\\pi}^\\infty\\frac{\\sin(1/x)}{x^2}\\,dx\n=\\int_0^\\pi\\sin u\\,du=[-\\cos u]_0^\\pi=2.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q26","number":26,"question":"<p>Using the substitution <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=2-x^3e^{-2x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and showing the limiting process used, show that</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{3/2}^\\infty\\frac{(3-2x)x^2}{x^3-2e^{2x}}\\,dx\n=\\ln\\left(\\frac{16e^3}{16e^3-27}\\right).\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p><strong>The following questions require A-Level Further Maths Integration methods, including Inverse Trigonometric Functions, Hyperbolic Functions and Partial Fractions.</strong></p>","steps":["<p>Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=2-x^3e^{-2x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Then <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"du=-(3-2x)x^2e^{-2x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{(3-2x)x^2}{x^3-2e^{2x}}\\,dx=\\frac{du}{u}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>The lower limit <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=3/2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=2-\\frac{27}{8e^3}=\\frac{16e^3-27}{8e^3}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, while <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u\\to2\" data-display=\"false\"></span> as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\\to\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{3/2}^\\infty\\frac{(3-2x)x^2}{x^3-2e^{2x}}\\,dx\n=\\ln2-\\ln\\left(\\frac{16e^3-27}{8e^3}\\right)\n=\\ln\\left(\\frac{16e^3}{16e^3-27}\\right).\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q27","number":27,"question":"<p>By showing the limiting process used, show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^1\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx=\\pi\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>The integrand is undefined at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\pm1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so split at zero and approach both endpoints by limits:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-1}^1\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to-1^+}\\int_a^0\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n+\\lim_{b\\to1^-}\\int_0^b\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n=\\frac\\pi2+\\frac\\pi2=\\pi.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q28","number":28,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty(\\cosh x-\\sinh x)\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty(\\cosh x-\\sinh x)\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}[\\sinh x-\\cosh x]_0^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}(1-e^{-a})=1.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q29","number":29,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find:</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1{4x^2-9}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li><li><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty\\frac1{4x^2+9}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span></p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>Using partial fractions,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac1{4x^2-9}=\\frac1{6(2x-3)}-\\frac1{6(2x+3)}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac1{4x^2-9}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[\\frac1{12}\\ln|2x-3|-\\frac1{12}\\ln|2x+3|\\right]_2^a\n=\\frac1{12}\\ln7.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac1{4x^2+9}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[\\frac16\\arctan\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right)\\right]_0^a\n=\\frac{\\pi}{12}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q30","number":30,"question":"<p>By showing the limiting process used, show that</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{12-6x}{(2x+3)(x^2+3)}\\,dx=\\ln k,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is a rational number to be found.</p>","steps":["<p>Partial fractions give</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{12-6x}{(2x+3)(x^2+3)}=\\frac4{2x+3}-\\frac{2x}{x^2+3}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Hence</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{12-6x}{(2x+3)(x^2+3)}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[2\\ln(2x+3)-\\ln(x^2+3)\\right]_0^a\n=\\ln4,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q31","number":31,"question":"<p>By showing the limiting process used, show that</p><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac{3x^2+16}{x^2(x^2+4)}\\,dx=2-k\\pi,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is a rational number to be found.</p>","steps":["<p>Partial fractions give</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{3x^2+16}{x^2(x^2+4)}=\\frac4{x^2}-\\frac1{x^2+4}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_2^\\infty\\frac{3x^2+16}{x^2(x^2+4)}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac4x-\\frac12\\arctan\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\right]_2^a\n=2-\\frac\\pi8,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k=1/8\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q32","number":32,"question":"<p>The diagram shown below shows the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle y=\\frac1{\\sqrt{x^2-1} }\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Find the exact shaded area between the curve, the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis, the line <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and the line <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/2.31/questions/assets/diagrams/q32-231.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>","steps":["<p>The shaded region is between <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=2\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The improper integral at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\" data-display=\"false\"></span> is convergent:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\nA=\\lim_{a\\to1^+}\\int_a^2\\frac1{\\sqrt{x^2-1}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to1^+}\\left[\\arcosh x\\right]_a^2\n=\\ln(2+\\sqrt3).\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q33","number":33,"question":"<p>The diagram shown below shows the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle y=\\frac1{x^2+8x+17}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Find the exact total shaded area between the curve and the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>-axis.</p><figure class=\"diagram\"><img src=\"assets/2.31/questions/assets/diagrams/q33-231.png\" alt=\"Original mathematical diagram\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure><p><strong>Using L'Hôpital's Rule</strong></p>","steps":["<p>Complete the square: <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2+8x+17=(x+4)^2+1\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. The total shaded area is</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{(x+4)^2+1}\n=\\lim_{a\\to-\\infty}\\left[\\arctan(x+4)\\right]_a^0\n+\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\left[\\arctan(x+4)\\right]_0^b\n=\\pi.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q34","number":34,"question":"<p>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\frac{\\ln x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx=-4\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^1\\frac{\\ln x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}\\int_a^1x^{-1/2}\\ln x\\,dx.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>By parts with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"u=\\ln x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"dv=x^{-1/2}dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, an antiderivative is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\sqrt{x}\\ln x-4\\sqrt{x}\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Hence the limit is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-4\" data-display=\"false\"></span> (since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sqrt a\\ln a\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>).</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q35","number":35,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^{12}\\ln x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^{12}\\ln x\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to0^+}[x\\ln x-x]_a^{12}\n=12\\ln12-12,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>using <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\lim_{a\\to0^+}a\\ln a=0\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q36","number":36,"question":"<p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty x^2e^{-3x}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p>","steps":["<p>Integrate by parts twice:</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int x^2e^{-3x}\\,dx=-\\frac13x^2e^{-3x}-\\frac29xe^{-3x}-\\frac2{27}e^{-3x}+c.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_0^\\infty x^2e^{-3x}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac13x^2e^{-3x}-\\frac29xe^{-3x}-\\frac2{27}e^{-3x}\\right]_0^a\n=\\frac2{27}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q37","number":37,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use L'Hôpital's Rule to find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{\\ln x}{x^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>By L'Hôpital's Rule,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\ln x}{x}\n=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{1/x}{1}\n=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><p>By parts, an antiderivative is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"-\\ln x/x-1/x\" data-display=\"false\"></span>. Therefore</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{\\ln x}{x^2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac{\\ln x}{x}-\\frac1x\\right]_1^a\n=1,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>using part a).</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q38","number":38,"question":"<ol class=\"parts\" start=\"1\"><li><p>Use L'Hôpital's Rule to find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\frac{x}{e^x}\\right)\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Showing the limiting process used, find <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^0xe^x\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span>.</p></li></ol>","steps":["<h3 class=\"part-label\">a)</h3><p>By L'Hôpital's Rule,</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{x}{e^x}\n=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac1{e^x}=0.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<h3 class=\"part-label\">b)</h3><span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_{-\\infty}^0xe^x\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to-\\infty}[xe^x-e^x]_a^0\n=-1-\\lim_{a\\to-\\infty}(ae^a-e^a)=-1,\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>since <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"e^a\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"ae^a=a/e^{-a}\\to0\" data-display=\"false\"></span> by part a).</p>"]},{"id":"fm-core2-2.31-q39","number":39,"question":"<p>Show clearly that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\displaystyle\\int_3^\\infty\\frac{\\ln x-1}{(\\ln x)^2}\\,dx\" data-display=\"false\"></span> diverges.</p>","steps":["<p>Observe that</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{x}{\\ln x}\\right)=\\frac{\\ln x-1}{(\\ln x)^2}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>Thus</p>","<span class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\n\\int_3^\\infty\\frac{\\ln x-1}{(\\ln x)^2}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\left[\\frac{x}{\\ln x}\\right]_3^a\n=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}\\frac{a}{\\ln a}-\\frac3{\\ln3}.\n\" data-display=\"true\"></span>","<p>By L'Hôpital's Rule, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}a/\\ln a=\\lim_{a\\to\\infty}1/(1/a)=+\\infty\" data-display=\"false\"></span>, so the integral diverges.</p>"]}]